          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Family and Succession</strong><br>\nPeisistratos had sons who were involved in his political legacy. His son Hippias succeeded him after his death and continued his policies. However, his other son Hipparchus was more interested in cultural pursuits. The rule of his sons led to increased discontent, which eventually resulted in their overthrow.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Influence</strong><br>\nPeisistratos also used religion to legitimize his rule by claiming divine support. He manipulated religious symbols and rituals to strengthen his position, which was a common strategy used by rulers of the time to justify their authority.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Peisistratos</strong><br>\nPeisistratos's legacy in Athens was marked by both his tyranny and his reforms. Although he was a dictator, his rule brought stability and prosperity to Athens. He laid the groundwork for the democratic reforms that would follow his death, and his influence on the city's culture and economy was lasting.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
